e('the kernel\'s per-packet work, not your application, becomes the bottleneck.','kernel per-packet work can become a bottleneck, depending on the application and workload.','Notuniversalbottleneck.',na)
e('The smallest Ethernet frame is 64 bytes, plus 20 bytes of preamble and inter-frame gap on the wire, so 84 bytes, or 672 bits.','For the standard untagged minimum-frame calculation, use 64 frame bytes including FCS, plus 8 bytes of preamble/SFD and a 12-byte-time inter-frame gap: 84 byte-times, or 672 bit-times. This is link occupancy, not all payload or frame bytes.','ExplicitEthernetoccupancymodel.',sc)
e('A cache miss to DRAM costs around 100 ns, and a system call more than that. At 10 Gbit/s with small packets, one core cannot afford even one of either per packet.','The serial time budget is small, but hardware latency is workload-dependent and multiple memory accesses can overlap. The table alone does not prove a fixed DRAM/syscall cost or a universal single-core throughput limit.','Removeunsupportedlatency andseriallatencyvs throughput conflation.',na)
