e('DPDK bypasses the kernel with user-space poll-mode drivers for maximum speed at the cost of dedicated cores and tooling; XDP runs verified eBPF code in the driver before skb allocation and can pass anything it doesn\'t handle to the normal stack.','Exclusive DPDK polling can bypass the normal kernel packet path, trading CPU/resources and tooling for a different processing model; bifurcated modes differ. Native XDP can reject packets before the normal skb path or pass them onward.','Noabsoluteperformance/architecture.',dp+xp)
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Implementation and measurement gaps\n> No reproducible DRAM/syscall-latency, DPDK/XDP per-core throughput or universal tuning benchmark accompanies this lesson, so fixed performance claims are omitted. The XDP fragment does not implement VLAN/IPv6 parsing, fragment reassembly or checksum validation; passing those cases is deliberate, not proof they are safe. Kernel budgets, offloads and driver modes must be checked on the actual system.\n\n## Further reading','Explicitlimits andomissions.',ver+dp+na)
f(1,'workedExample','Using the supplied occupancy model, 84 × 8 =672 bit-times. At10^10 bits/s, spacing is672/10^10 seconds =67.2 ns, corresponding to about14.88 million frames/s. This is aggregate arrival spacing; memory-level parallelism, batching, offloads and multiple cores determine processing capacity. No fixed DRAM/syscall latency is implied.','Throughputnotseriallatencyproof.',sc)
f(2,'explanation','Correct for the conventional skb-based stack. Drivers may build, reuse or allocate these structures as appropriate, while native XDP drops can occur before that path.','Notstrictnewallocationperframe.',xp,2)
f(3,'prompt','In the conventional interrupt-driven softirq NAPI path, what happens after a receive notification while the queue remains busy?','Threaded/busypollmode exceptions.',na)
f(3,'explanation','NAPI schedules receive processing; packets can also be dropped for policy, malformed headers, allocation failures and other reasons, not only queue overflow.','Dropsnotonlyqueuesfull.',na,1)
f(4,'text','CPU0 is repeatedly reaching the receive poll budget/time condition; investigate queue placement, processing cost and workload before tuning','Countersnotexactbacklogproof.',src,1)
f(4,'explanation','Correct. time_squeeze records the relevant budget/time test. It suggests receive-processing pressure but does not alone prove packets remain in a hardware ring or identify a misconfiguration.','Sourcecountersemantics.',src,1)
f(4,'explanation','A slow application can cause other queueing/drop symptoms and can coexist with processing pressure. This counter specifically records the receive-poll budget/time condition; it does not identify the application as the cause.','Symptomsnotmutuallyexclusive.',src,3)
f(4,'workedExample','1. Identify CPU rows using the running kernel\'s softnet_stat format; row position is not always CPU ID when CPUs are offline.\n2. Correlate rising time_squeeze with actual budget values, CPU utilization, drop counters, IRQ affinity and queue configuration.\n3. More budget can lengthen uninterrupted processing and affect other work. More queues or RPS can add cross-core overhead.\n4. Choose changes from measured evidence rather than treating the counter as a complete diagnosis.','Nounconditionaltunefix.',src+sc)
f(5,'prompt','Assume stable conventional RSS across8 queues, balanced mapping for many independent flows, fixed IRQ affinity and no RPS/RFS. Why can one TCP backup flow still concentrate receive processing on one CPU?','Excludeambiguousbadmapping.',sc)
f(5,'workedExample','1. A stable flow tuple hashes to one table bucket and RX queue.\n2. In the stated configuration, that queue is handled on one CPU.\n3. Additional independent streams can hash to other queues, but collisions are possible. Eight streams do not imply eight distinct queues.\n4. In an ideal independent uniform model, eight streams across eight queues occupy on average8 × (1 − (7/8)^8) ≈5.25 queues; real hashes/configuration can differ.','Originallikely8distinctfalse;illustrativemodel.',sc)
f(6,'text','XPS can select a transmit queue using CPU or receive-queue mappings','RXqueueXPSsupport.',sc,2)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. Appropriate queue mappings can reduce contention and improve locality; no universal zero-contention guarantee follows.','Benefitconditional.',sc,2)
f(8,'prompt','A native-mode XDP program returns XDP_PASS for a packet. What happens next in the normal receive path?','GenericalreadyhasSKB.',xp)
f(8,'text','The packet proceeds toward the normal kernel networking stack, subject to subsequent processing and possible drops','AsifXDPneverranfalseifmodified.',xp,1)
f(8,'explanation','Correct. PASS permits stack processing; it does not guarantee socket delivery or undo modifications the XDP program made.','PASSnotdeliveryguarantee.',xp,1)
f(9,'prompt','Profiling shows costly normal-stack processing of unwanted UDP traffic. Assume native XDP support and a correct classifier, while HTTPS must remain on the normal kernel stack. Which option directly removes early per-packet work for the unwanted traffic?','Noalwayscheapestfromincompletefacts.',xp)
f(9,'explanation','An exclusive VFIO DPDK design would remove that data path from the normal stack; bifurcated/coexistence designs also exist, so DPDK does not universally take an entire NIC away.','Bifurcatedexception.',dp,0)
f(9,'explanation','Generic XDP retains skb allocation costs. It can still help some workloads; its benefit is not universally negligible or limited to testing.','Genericnotonlytest.',xp,1)
f(9,'explanation','Correct under the assumptions. Native XDP can reject before normal skb/stack processing, retaining a PASS path for legitimate packets. Classification and processing still cost CPU.','Notalmostzero.',xp,2)
f(9,'workedExample','Normal netfilter drops occur later than native XDP receive classification. Native XDP can discard matched unwanted packets before normal skb/stack work while returning PASS for legitimate traffic. Benchmark the actual classifier, driver, packet mix and mode; this is a path-cost argument, not proof of a particular packet rate or current vendor deployment.','Removeunsupportedproductiongeneralization.',xp)
f(10,'prompt','For a conventional exclusive VFIO DPDK application using dedicated always-busy poll loops and no kernel-stack integration, which tradeoffs apply? Select all that apply.','Restricttoarchitectureactuallyhascosts.',dp)
f(10,'explanation','Correct in the stated exclusive data-path model: ordinary kernel-stack packet tools do not see that bypassed traffic. The kernel still manages the VFIO device; DPDK-specific capture and other modes can differ.','Kernelseesdevice.',dp,1)
f(10,'workedExample','The stated design avoids ordinary per-packet syscall/skb processing and dedicates CPU polling time. Its bypassed traffic is outside normal kernel-stack tooling, and TCP requires an appropriate stack/integration. DPDK does not universally require these choices: bifurcated drivers, alternative PMDs and power-management modes change the tradeoffs.','ScopeallDPDKclaim.',dp)
